    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d622">

                <pb facs="0595" n="583" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg583"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.516/171: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dreyfus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-07-16T13:00:00+03:25">July 16, 1943—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 17—10:54 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>727. Department’s 226, May 7.<anchor n="99" xml:id="d622-f1"/> Millspaugh and
                    Director of Banque Mellie request Department to endeavor to arrange for the
                    conversion into gold and shipment to Iran by air of one million dollars <pb
                        facs="0596" n="584" xml:id="pg584"/> of Iranian Government deposit in
                    Guaranty Trust Company terms and conditions to be similar to those of previous
                    shipment. Cable reply requested.</p>
                <p>For the Department’s information British sales of gold in Iran are proceeding
                    sucessfully. Daily sales are limited to 8,000 sovereigns although there is
                    demand for more. Rate today is 550 rials per sovereign. Since present British
                    stock of 100,000 sovereigns will soon be exhausted additional 200,000 are being
                    brought from Cairo. Bazaar reports point to steadying of prices, perhaps partly
                    as result of gold sales. Bar gold imported by Iranian Government from United
                    States is being minted into gold pahlevis for sale under similar conditions as
                    British coins.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dreyfus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
